+# List most recently created links: `GET /api/v1/links`
+
+## Overview
+
+`GET /api/v1/links` returns the most recently created short links, newest first, as a bare JSON array. An optional `limit` query parameter bounds the result size. It defaults to 20 and accepts values from 1 to 100. Each array item has exactly the same JSON shape as the body of `GET /api/v1/links/{code}`.
+
+## Requirements traceability
+
+| ID | Summary | Where it is covered in this document |
+|----|---------|--------------------------------------|
+| FR-1 | `GET /api/v1/links` returns 200 with a bare JSON array, newest first, and `[]` when empty. Existing endpoints are unchanged. | [Successful response](#successful-response-fr-1-fr-2-fr-4), [Ordering](#ordering-fr-1) |
+| FR-2 | Optional `limit`: default 20, range 1..100. The response holds at most `limit` items, which are the newest. | [Query parameters](#query-parameters-fr-2-fr-3), [Limit semantics](#limit-semantics-fr-2) |
+| FR-3 | An out-of-range `limit` returns 400 with an RFC 9457 problem detail through the existing error handling. | [Error responses](#error-responses-fr-3) |
+| FR-4 | Each item is JSON-equal to the `GET /api/v1/links/{code}` body and uses the same representation. | [Item shape](#item-shape-fr-4) |
+| FR-5 | The list contains exactly the links retrievable via `GET /api/v1/links/{code}`, under the same access control and with no ownership filtering. | [Visibility and access](#visibility-and-access-fr-5) |
+
+## Endpoint
+
+```
+GET /api/v1/links
+```
+
+- **Authentication:** none. The endpoint is exactly as open as `GET /api/v1/links/{code}`, which is unauthenticated in this service (FR-5).
+- **Success content type:** `application/json`
+- **Error content type:** `application/problem+json`
+
+### Query parameters (FR-2, FR-3)
+
+| Name | Type | Required | Default | Valid values |
+|------|------|----------|---------|--------------|
+| `limit` | integer | no | `20` | `1` to `100` inclusive |
+
+The default of 20 applies only when the parameter is **absent**. An empty value (`?limit=`) is rejected; it does not fall back to the default.
+
+## Successful response (FR-1, FR-2, FR-4)
+
+The response is HTTP `200 OK` with a top-level JSON array. There is no envelope, no wrapper object and no metadata.
+
+Request:
+
+```http
+GET /api/v1/links?limit=2 HTTP/1.1
+Accept: application/json
+```
+
+Response:
+
+```http
+HTTP/1.1 200 OK
+Content-Type: application/json
+
+[
+  { /* body of GET /api/v1/links/{code} for the newest link */ },
+  { /* body of GET /api/v1/links/{code} for the second-newest link */ }
+]
+```
+
+> The individual item fields are deliberately not repeated here. Each item is the exact `GET /api/v1/links/{code}` response body (see [Item shape](#item-shape-fr-4)). Refer to that endpoint's documentation for the field list.
+
+When no links exist:
+
+```http
+HTTP/1.1 200 OK
+Content-Type: application/json
+
+[]
+```
+
+### Ordering (FR-1)
+
+- Items are ordered by creation time, newest first.
+- For any two adjacent items, the first item's creation time is greater than or equal to the second's.
+- "Most recently created" means creation time. Last access and update times are not used.
+- Links with identical creation timestamps are ordered by a stable secondary key (the link's primary key, descending), so results are deterministic. Clients should not depend on this tie-break.
+
+### Limit semantics (FR-2)
+
+| Visible links | Request | Result |
+|---------------|---------|--------|
+| 25 | `GET /api/v1/links` | The 20 newest |
+| 25 | `GET /api/v1/links?limit=5` | The 5 newest, newest first |
+| 3 | `GET /api/v1/links?limit=10` | All 3 |
+| any | `GET /api/v1/links?limit=1` | At most 1 |
+| 150 | `GET /api/v1/links?limit=100` | The 100 newest |
+
+### Item shape (FR-4)
+
+Each array element is built with the same response representation used by `GET /api/v1/links/{code}` (`LinkResponse`, created via `LinkResponse.from(link, properties.baseUrl())`). For any link `L` in the list, the item for `L` is JSON-equal to the body of a successful `GET /api/v1/links/{L.code}` in the same data state. Any future change to that representation shows up in both endpoints.
+
+### Visibility and access (FR-5)
+
+The list contains exactly the links for which `GET /api/v1/links/{code}` succeeds.
+
+- `GET /api/v1/links/{code}` returns every persisted link, **including deactivated and expired links**. It fails only when the link does not exist (404).
+- The list therefore applies **no** visibility filter: deactivated and expired links appear in it.
+- Because no link is excluded, `limit` applies to the full set of links. This still satisfies the rule that the limit applies after visibility filtering.
+- There is no per-caller or owner-based filtering. All callers receive the same list for the same data state.
+- There is no authentication on either endpoint. Any caller that can reach `GET /api/v1/links/{code}` can reach `GET /api/v1/links`.
+
+An item's presence in the list says nothing about whether its short URL currently redirects. Check the item's own fields, or the redirect endpoint, for that.
+
+## Error responses (FR-3)
+
+Invalid `limit` values are rejected with HTTP `400 Bad Request` and an RFC 9457 problem detail. The response is produced by the service's existing `ProblemDetail` error handling, and no new error format is introduced. A rejected request returns no link data and has no side effects: validation happens before any data access.
+
+| Input | Result | Mechanism |
+|-------|--------|-----------|
+| `?limit=0` | 400 | Range check (existing `InvalidUrlException` mapping) |
+| `?limit=101` | 400 | Range check (existing `InvalidUrlException` mapping) |
+| `?limit=-1` | 400 | Range check (existing `InvalidUrlException` mapping) |
+| `?limit=` (empty) | 400 | Explicit empty-value check (existing `InvalidUrlException` mapping) |
+| `?limit=abc` | 400 | Type conversion failure (existing `MethodArgumentTypeMismatchException` handling) |
+| `?limit=1.5` | 400 | Type conversion failure (existing `MethodArgumentTypeMismatchException` handling) |
+
+Request:
+
+```http
+GET /api/v1/links?limit=0 HTTP/1.1
+```
+
+Response:
+
+```http
+HTTP/1.1 400 Bad Request
+Content-Type: application/problem+json
+
+{
+  "status": 400,
+  "title": "<title set by the existing error handler>"
+}
+```
+
+The body always contains at least `status` and `title`. The exact `title`, `detail` and any other members are those produced by the existing handler for the exception involved, so they are not fixed here. Clients should rely on the HTTP status and `status` member, not on message text.
+
+## Unchanged endpoints
+
+The following endpoints are not changed: `POST /api/v1/links`, `GET /api/v1/links/{code}`, `DELETE /api/v1/links/{code}`, `GET /api/v1/links/{code}/stats` and `GET /{code}`. Existing tests are unmodified.
+
+## Limitations
+
+- **No pagination.** There is no offset, cursor, next-page link or total count. At most the 100 newest links can be retrieved, and older links are not reachable through this endpoint.
+- **No consistent snapshot.** Two calls may return different results if links are created between them. Clients cannot page through results with repeated calls.
+- **No filtering, search or alternative sort orders.** The order is always newest first by creation time.
+- **Deactivated and expired links are included** (see FR-5). Clients that want only active links must filter on the client side using the item fields.
+- **Unauthenticated and unscoped.** Any caller can list the newest links across all users, mirroring `GET /api/v1/links/{code}`. Operators should take this into account, because it makes recent links enumerable.
+- **No endpoint-specific caching or rate limiting.** The existing `RateLimitFilter` was not modified for this endpoint.
+- **No supporting index.** No migration was added. `ORDER BY created_at DESC, id DESC LIMIT n` may become slower on large `links` tables. A `links(created_at DESC, id DESC)` index is a candidate follow-up.
+- **Fixed bounds.** The default (20) and maximum (100) are not configurable.
+
+## Maintainer notes
+
+### Implementation
+
+- **`LinkRepository`:** adds the derived query `findAllByOrderByCreatedAtDescIdDesc(org.springframework.data.domain.Limit)`.
+  - The database applies ordering and `LIMIT`, so at most `limit` rows are fetched.
+  - No `COUNT` query runs.
+  - There is no visibility predicate.
+- **`LinkService.listRecent(int)`:** validates `1 <= limit <= 100` before any repository access and throws the existing `InvalidUrlException` on violation. This follows the convention used for `days` in `AnalyticsService`.
+- **`LinkController`:** adds a `@GetMapping` on the existing `/api/v1/links` base path.
+  - `limit` is bound as an optional `Integer` with no `defaultValue`.
+  - If the raw parameter is absent, 20 is used.
+  - If it is present but empty, `InvalidUrlException` is thrown.
+  - Non-integer values fail binding and go through the existing type-mismatch handling.
+  - Items are mapped with `LinkResponse.from(link, properties.baseUrl())`, the same call as the `GET /{code}` handler.
+- **Not changed:** `GlobalExceptionHandler`, `LinkResponse`, schema/migrations and security configuration (none exists in the codebase).
+
+**Why `InvalidUrlException` and not `ResponseStatusException` or `@Min`/`@Max`:** `InvalidUrlException` is already mapped to a 400 problem detail. The alternatives are not shown to be handled by `GlobalExceptionHandler` and could fall into its catch-all handler as a 500.
+
+**Visibility coupling:** the list intentionally applies no filter, because `LinkService.get()` returns every persisted link. If `GET /api/v1/links/{code}` ever starts rejecting some links (for example, returning 404 or 410 for expired links), this list query must gain the same predicate to keep FR-5 parity.
+
+### Files changed
+
+- `src/main/java/io/agentic/shortener/repository/LinkRepository.java`
+- `src/main/java/io/agentic/shortener/service/LinkService.java`
+- `src/main/java/io/agentic/shortener/web/LinkController.java`
+- `src/test/java/io/agentic/shortener/repository/LinkRepositoryRecentLinksTest.java`
+- `src/test/java/io/agentic/shortener/web/LinkListAccessParityTest.java`
+- `src/test/java/io/agentic/shortener/web/LinkListApiIntegrationTest.java`
+
+### Tests
+
+- **`LinkRepositoryRecentLinksTest`** covers:
+  - ordering by `createdAt` rather than insertion order
+  - the deterministic tie-break
+  - the bounded row count
+  - the empty result
+  - inclusion of inactive and expired links
+- **`LinkListAccessParityTest`** checks that the list endpoint gets the same unauthenticated 200 as `GET /api/v1/links/{code}`.
+- **`LinkListApiIntegrationTest`** (MockMvc) covers:
+  - array shape and ordering, including non-increasing `createdAt`
+  - the empty `[]` result
+  - default, explicit, `1` and `100` limits
+  - rejection of `0`, `101`, `-1`, `abc`, `1.5` and an empty value, each with no link data and no side effects
+  - JSON equality with `GET /api/v1/links/{code}`
+  - visibility parity: 30 links with the 10 newest deactivated or expired, and `limit=20`
+  - identical results for two callers
+
+**Note for reviewers:** the design (D6) called for a single integration test class. The implementation adds three test classes, including a repository-level test. Confirm this deviation is acceptable.
